### Per-sorry verdict

**1. `isUMP_oneSided` (line 412), sorry = `α ∈ {0,1}` endpoint branch — FALSE at corner.**
Main case `0 < α < 1` is fully proven in-file. The endpoint is unattainable: `exists_critical_constants` needs `0<α<1`, and for `𝒩(θ,1)`, `T=id`, size `= 1−Φ(C−θ₀) ∈ (0,1)` for every real `C`, so exact size `0` or `1` is impossible. Counterexample already correctly documented.

**2. `power_strictMono_oneSided` (line 484) — TRUE but BLOCKED (lifted).**
The `≤` direction is `integral_mono_of_hasMLR`. Strictness is the *strict* half of MP-test unbiasedness — literally the content of **`NeymanPearson.Lemma.power_gt_alpha_of_ne`, which is an open `sorry` upstream** (I confirmed it at `Lemma.lean:519`). A self-contained proof needs a measure-theoretic association/FKG correlation inequality plus a "ratio-constant-on-`T`-level-sets" lemma; **Mathlib v4.29.1 has no such inequality** (only the finite-sum `Algebra/Order/Chebyshev.lean`). Not faked.

**3. `isUMP_oneSided_shifted` (line 515), sorry = degenerate branch (`p θ' = 0` on `{T≥C}`) — FALSE.**
Verified counterexample: `𝓧=ℝ`, `μ=`Lebesgue, `T=id`, reflected shifted-exponential `p θ x = exp(x−θ)·1_{x≤θ}` (genuine MLR), `C=θ'+1`. Then `power P φ θ' = 0`, but the level-`0` competitor `ψ = 1_{x>θ'}` gives `power ψ θ'' = 1−e^{θ'−θ''} > 1−e^{θ'+1−θ''} = power P φ θ''` for all `θ''>θ'+1`. So `ψ` strictly beats `φ`; `φ` is **not** UMP.

**4. `power_min_oneSided` (line 675), sorry = degenerate branch (`p θ₀ = 0` on `{T≤C}`) — FALSE.**
Verified counterexample: shifted-exponential `p θ x = exp(−(x−θ))·1_{x≥θ}` (genuine MLR), `C=θ₀−1` (forces `α=1`). Competitor `ψ = 1_{x≥θ₀}` has `power ψ θ₀ = 1 = α` but `power ψ θ = e^{θ−θ₀} < e^{θ−θ₀+1} = power P φ θ` for every `θ<θ₀−1`. So `φ` does **not** minimize rejection probability.

**5. `essentiallyComplete_oneSidedTest` (line 734) — FALSE (full statement).**
Verified counterexample: `ψ = 1` (reject always) is a critical function with `power P ψ θ₀ = 1`. With `𝒩(θ,1)`, `T=id`, `θ₀=0`, `L₀=0`, `L₁ θ = −θ` (satisfies both loss hypotheses), any **real-`C`** one-sided test has `power P φ θ = 1−Φ(C−θ) < 1`, so for every `θ>0`, `testRisk φ θ = power φ θ·(−θ) > 1·(−θ) = testRisk ψ θ`. No real-`C` one-sided test dominates `ψ`. The classical class is essentially complete only when the trivial `C=±∞` tests are admitted — which the frozen real-`C` signature (per its own docstring note "`C`… is a real number, not an extended one") excludes.

